{B}$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535473 false] [17592186124220 1100 "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Find the values of $a,b$, and $c$ if\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a & 2 \\\\ -3 & 7 & b \\\\ c & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535473 false] [17592186124220 1100 "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/827f1a79a51ea88e59f5d766ee531cacf3945cc2ce649521c7b1d8e7eea35e06.png)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/82d0eab6a51dbf1615a3e2bb4fc6ffef622a38ac3c980dc742b552946c11a87a.png)" 13194139535473 true] [17592186124220 1145 "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/0d9a23830f48d4659dd963110d9322c12e8d4849bd9bcd63ae9d5adb0e1fba05.png)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/10b5f2e35da56c7be6d547827164c68f475df4d012116b9666c12a94bbc39f55.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the c